(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{
Cell["Why Boson Sampling Is Hard", "Title",
 CellID->874461601,ExpressionUUID->"22a1a762-10e3-861e-525e-3f4ca097ed64"],

Cell["\<\
Identical Photons, Permanents, and the One Sign That Separates Easy from \
Hard: A Short, Computation-First Argument with the Wolfram Language and \
QuantumFramework\
\>", "Subtitle",
 CellID->36768919,ExpressionUUID->"4da796ef-9f57-a4bf-7b5c-0fc91ae30e85"],

Cell["Mads Bahrami (last updated: Oct 12, 2026)", "Author",
 CellID->1699946459,ExpressionUUID->"1b39f900-6440-cee7-4ac6-f16a0dfa94cd"],

Cell["Wolfram Research Inc, USA", "Affiliation",
 CellID->1535504786,ExpressionUUID->"75d93363-5da6-51a4-23e6-ab4d5453238e"],

Cell["Setting the Stage: How This Notebook Flows", "Subsection",
 CellID->1208482393,ExpressionUUID->"25a568a6-87a2-527f-535a-810032572145"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This notebook makes one argument, and only one: when identical photons pass \
through an optical network, the probability of each detection pattern is a \
permanent of a matrix, and a permanent is expensive to compute, so a \
classical computer cannot keep up with the photons. We will send a few \
photons into a random interferometer, read the distribution of outcomes \
straight from ",
 Cell[BoxData["QuantumInterferometer"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "00d7f70d-8d0e-ae34-30ec-ef5724b8f51e"],
 ", show that each probability is a permanent, time that permanent as photons \
are added, and finish with the one small difference (a sign) that makes the \
fermionic cousin of the same problem easy."
}], "Text",
 CellID->28909615,ExpressionUUID->"2f3e8453-9762-68ea-56c5-1e1d22173a30"],

Cell["\<\
In other words, I want to earn the claim \"boson sampling is hard\" by \
computing it, not by repeating it. I strongly believe in a computation-first \
narrative for learning: in a sense, if I cannot compute it, I cannot claim to \
understand it. Everything below is run, not asserted.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->193832008,ExpressionUUID->"5902d484-617f-9570-0f31-cdee100bfc5a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Before we start, a few things. The environment you see is a ",
 ButtonBox["Wolfram (Mathematica) notebook",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://www.wolfram.com/notebooks/"], None}],
 ". Evaluate the cells from top to bottom; a few later cells use a network \
and a distribution defined earlier, so order matters. Two cells in the second \
half time a computation on purpose and take up to about a minute; only their \
trend matters, not the absolute seconds on your machine."
}], "Text",
 CellID->684317082,ExpressionUUID->"56e0e12c-de7d-6135-7e9f-f889db08301f"],

Cell["\<\
The story runs as a continuous sequence, like a movie, with a few headings \
only as transitions. My suggestion is to read each output and its meaning \
first, and worry about how the input cell is written afterward. And remember \
you are not locked into the code: change the number of photons, the number of \
modes, the seed, and watch the argument bend or hold.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->528485616,ExpressionUUID->"5708d032-2150-04f2-7d37-ca7c50e56ed8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If you have any suggestions or questions, please reach out to us at ",
 ButtonBox["quantum@wolfram.com",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["mailto:quantum@wolfram.com"], None}]
}], "Text",
 CellID->1392207227,ExpressionUUID->"3b7807e8-9504-b25a-6907-9eb21cb600a0"],

Cell["Prerequisites and How to Read This", "Subsection",
 CellID->705466992,ExpressionUUID->"166f8969-5e31-e8e5-3e14-21335bc5bacf"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "You only need to be comfortable with the idea of photons occupying modes: a \
state ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox[
       StyleBox["n", "TI"], "1"], ",", 
      SubscriptBox[
       StyleBox["n", "TI"], "2"], ",", "\[Ellipsis]"}]},
   "Ket"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "234f1fb7-07c6-bba9-5174-96f1b27009f3"],
 " says how many photons sit in each mode, and a detector reports one such \
pattern. Everything optical is handled by ",
 Cell[BoxData["QuantumInterferometer"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6ee45b93-a0df-d1f6-169c-52dcd16ba3dc"],
 ", a QuantumFramework tool that builds a passive optical network and hands \
back its outcome probabilities directly. The combinatorial objects at the \
end, the permanent and the determinant, are built into the Wolfram Language."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1350516518,ExpressionUUID->"0a3edd72-e0d3-62fb-3bc5-d53f916630c1"],

Cell["Let's start!", "Text",
 CellID->907546795,ExpressionUUID->"6ec9a758-58be-624c-16f6-3f02594b3096"],

Cell["A Boson Sampler: Photons Into a Random Network", "Section",
 CellID->814772321,ExpressionUUID->"23253b8d-1c99-a6bf-52da-b2d7952d0c85"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A passive optical network is a box of mirrors, beam splitters and phase \
shifters: it moves photons between modes without creating, destroying or \
interacting them. A boson sampler feeds ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6e84829b-9667-6594-16bb-1bc523124ffe"],
 " identical photons into such a box, one per input port, and lets detectors \
at the output report which modes the photons left in. In other words, the \
machine's whole output is a list of detection patterns, drawn with whatever \
probabilities the physics assigns."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1054628905,ExpressionUUID->"569e173f-6b36-b240-7ea5-8f790ec000aa"],

Cell["\<\
Load QuantumFramework together with its second-quantization sub-package, \
where the optical tools live:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->2043779557,ExpressionUUID->"15a3e88e-0b57-fa6e-4350-01e8aee3c8b4"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<Wolfram`QuantumFramework`\>\"", "]"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<Wolfram`QuantumFramework`SecondQuantization`\>\"",
   "]"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[1]:= ",
 CellID->17830494,ExpressionUUID->"779c57de-bb93-d239-1da4-4e983e27a103"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The hardness argument wants a network with no special structure, so we take \
one at random, a unitary drawn uniformly over all optical networks of its \
size. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"QuantumInterferometer", "[", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<Random\>\"", "[", "m", "]"}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2867ea9b-6e8d-517b-581c-03c50b382041"],
 " builds such an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["m", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "76dfbc62-92ac-7b6e-1ee4-364c275949b4"],
 "-mode network and factors it into beam splitters and phase shifters for \
us."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1589986527,ExpressionUUID->"0df4b6a5-173c-e15a-3b8b-2f0fa2ceb3a0"],

Cell["Build a random five-mode interferometer with a fixed seed:", "Text",
 CellID->943101875,ExpressionUUID->"4d620fc7-5cf2-d9d8-7b11-a6a15484ab22"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"SeedRandom", "[", "2026", "]"}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"qi", " ", "=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"QuantumInterferometer", "[", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<Random\>\"", "[", "5", "]"}], "]"}]}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[2]:= ",
 CellID->67888809,ExpressionUUID->"741b091d-feb2-1a39-1c38-a146fb476903"],

Cell["\<\
Draw the network, so we can see the mesh of beam splitters and phase shifters \
the photons will meet:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1432876021,ExpressionUUID->"3bf5a20e-c541-e06e-698a-446becf5b2b4"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"qi", "[", "\"\<Diagram\>\"", "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[3]:= ",
 CellID->876231958,ExpressionUUID->"07964fa7-8d1e-ff14-2dae-660124a8c27e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Now send three photons in, one into each of the first three ports, and ask \
for the probability of every detection pattern at the output. ",
 Cell[BoxData["QuantumInterferometer"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3e367b18-9aa8-58f9-67ce-73421f5d2ac3"],
 " returns the whole distribution at once, keyed by the output pattern in \
Dirac notation."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1020575516,ExpressionUUID->"1dbaaaf7-1388-e538-4b59-43b1a622cc02"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Collect the outcome distribution for the input ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{"1,1,1,0,0"},
   "Ket"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "77d99fdb-a351-2d53-1de6-1684d6e477b9"],
 ":"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1967685850,ExpressionUUID->"04646437-faee-7dfc-2c1c-7e70fe9b47c6"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"probs", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"qi", "[", 
    RowBox[{"\"\<Probabilities\>\"", ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
      "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "0", ",", " ", "0"}], 
      "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[4]:= ",
 CellID->1044562475,ExpressionUUID->"1cbde497-e826-5557-4442-fdd089d33fbd"],

Cell["Show the five most likely detection patterns:", "Text",
 CellID->1653200050,ExpressionUUID->"6ecb37d0-fbcc-3e82-16c8-ae9dfe7e84e8"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"TakeLargest", "[", 
  RowBox[{"probs", ",", " ", "5"}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[5]:= ",
 CellID->1384978530,ExpressionUUID->"4c358d3e-86d3-ec5f-75ca-25783367b6a5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The sampler's job is to produce patterns with exactly these probabilities. \
There is one number for every way to place three photons in five modes, which \
is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    RowBox[{
      StyleBox["n", "TI"], "+", 
      StyleBox["m", "TI"], "-", "1"}], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]},
   "Binomial"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1f908cf3-3c70-d55a-45af-25bdb50abfa0"],
 " patterns."
}], "Text",
 CellID->519689844,ExpressionUUID->"5dac66ba-272d-3627-0b54-7fe452d99d0d"],

Cell["\<\
Confirm that the distribution has one probability per pattern:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1609367578,ExpressionUUID->"110914de-bbdd-83a1-4736-8d983e69d18b"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Length", "[", "probs", "]"}], " ", "==", " ", 
  RowBox[{"Binomial", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"3", " ", "+", " ", "5", " ", "-", " ", "1"}], ",", " ", "3"}], 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[6]:= ",
 CellID->1541450781,ExpressionUUID->"76d004f9-29e5-6665-1eef-9da34f904d4f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Confirm that it is a genuine probability distribution, summing to one (",
 Cell[BoxData["Chop"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2c76c797-c249-79ca-540d-ded0f7fc4b10"],
 " sets the tiny machine-precision residue to zero so the comparison tests \
the physics, not the rounding):"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1657269721,ExpressionUUID->"35077f55-9291-6519-633d-771f27244c63"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Chop", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"Total", "[", "probs", "]"}], " ", "-", " ", "1"}], "]"}], " ", "==",
   " ", "0"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[7]:= ",
 CellID->1140508347,ExpressionUUID->"4ebf3687-f906-130d-7644-afe0f8b37e57"],

Cell["\<\
So far this is just a machine producing a distribution. The real question is \
where these numbers come from, and that is where the permanent appears.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->496109794,ExpressionUUID->"65de4a87-3471-cd9f-13e6-63e1bd0597e5"],

Cell["Every Probability Is a Permanent", "Section",
 CellID->108435171,ExpressionUUID->"7bedd9bd-1d84-ddaa-2992-d0e79436a7f0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Everything the network does to a single photon is held in one ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["m", "TI"], "\[Times]", 
   StyleBox["m", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4c37f35a-e1a6-07fe-75cc-eb0790536dc4"],
 " matrix ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["W", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "02f88238-86c8-66df-3287-2462337d4c25"],
 ", its transfer matrix: the entry ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SubscriptBox[
   StyleBox["W", "TI"], 
   StyleBox[
    RowBox[{"j", "k"}], "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "034d641c-5834-e412-3173-7e4659c6cf78"],
 " is the amplitude for a photon that enters port ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["k", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "52ce4f6a-032d-a48b-02f6-6734b6d90ed1"],
 " to leave port ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["j", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5bfe104a-50de-a555-0985-8a1461680fbf"],
 ". In other words, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["W", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "134534fb-1061-d656-417a-ada5a115bcbc"],
 " is the network seen by one photon at a time."
}], "Text",
 CellID->829388421,ExpressionUUID->"2cd98fd2-34e7-e188-54e6-269cbc93b3d2"],

Cell["Read the transfer matrix of our network:", "Text",
 CellID->203008389,ExpressionUUID->"6e1c99a5-dcef-9c28-1623-100ed49be772"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"W", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"qi", "[", "\"\<TransferMatrix\>\"", "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{"W", " ", "//", " ", "MatrixForm"}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[8]:= ",
 CellID->1090263105,ExpressionUUID->"608e87cc-8fce-afdf-10b5-1e962a78f525"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "With several identical photons, the amplitude for a detection pattern is \
not one entry of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["W", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2aa912c6-4938-1bb7-5a56-8ba068cd699d"],
 " but a combination of many, one for every way of routing the photons to the \
detected modes. That combination is the permanent. The permanent of a square \
matrix is defined like the determinant, as a sum over all ways of pairing \
rows with columns, but with every term added with a plus sign (the \
determinant alternates the signs). Build the relevant submatrix by taking the \
rows and columns of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["W", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "66b4bee0-d247-b105-0e4b-37cde7f3006f"],
 " for the occupied output and input modes."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1560876240,ExpressionUUID->"337c2d89-644e-5b5e-6103-c4d30dfb29a4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Compute, directly from ",
 Cell[BoxData["QuantumInterferometer"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5f7c2741-1c81-ec9b-0503-bf2b9535b6e1"],
 ", the amplitude for all three photons to come out one per port again, in \
modes 1, 2 and 3:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->176337227,ExpressionUUID->"644cc522-c7c4-9b18-0c73-dc8ff276e662"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"qi", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<Amplitude\>\"", ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
     "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "0", ",", " ", "0"}], "}"}],
     " ", "->", " ", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
     "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "0", ",", " ", "0"}], 
     "}"}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[9]:= ",
 CellID->807053435,ExpressionUUID->"2de8c8f9-eaf3-8e90-5b97-e1a28e87d4fa"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "That single number is the permanent of the three-by-three block of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["W", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6801f052-da94-0b6f-183e-ea1fdf2159b5"],
 " on the occupied modes."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1633675818,ExpressionUUID->"0daf8e98-7628-e40b-3b54-27c203c2cf51"],

Cell["Confirm that the amplitude equals that permanent exactly:", "Text",
 CellID->1828793009,ExpressionUUID->"7b6211f5-475c-1194-2911-88bf72e963fe"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"qi", "[", 
   RowBox[{"\"\<Amplitude\>\"", ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
      "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "0", ",", " ", "0"}], 
      "}"}], " ", "->", " ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
      "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "0", ",", " ", "0"}], 
      "}"}]}]}], "]"}], " ", "===", " ", 
  RowBox[{"Permanent", "[", 
   RowBox[{"W", "[", 
    RowBox[{"[", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "2", ",", " ", "3"}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
      RowBox[{"{", 
       RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "2", ",", " ", "3"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}], 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[10]:= ",
 CellID->1232986364,ExpressionUUID->"7c8f7bdf-2493-8d8a-24b5-72994d27d7d0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This is not special to that one pattern. For a general input ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["S", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "14e8dcaf-74c9-37ba-3c97-d5097d7d9d80"],
 " and output ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["T", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5d5cbd3a-e88d-ee42-0b63-35678939b4a8"],
 ", the rule is the same: repeat row ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["j", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3b7fe4cc-5889-7f4e-6904-fd96593c4594"],
 " of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["W", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6cb5c01f-62f5-68e0-144a-da5916803aca"],
 " once for every photon leaving mode ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["j", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "55f417ec-7166-d78d-038b-8eb60010bdd7"],
 ", repeat column ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["k", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4b6e5dad-9702-0c1f-7916-54f722b75765"],
 " once for every photon entering mode ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["k", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1d359769-845f-cd94-44ca-0f332deb97fe"],
 ", take the permanent of that matrix, and divide by a normalization that \
counts photons sharing a mode."
}], "Text",
 CellID->762269937,ExpressionUUID->"2055bfee-d4b5-7aa2-506a-36cbdd404888"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Define the list that repeats mode ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["j", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "64c30c0d-1857-1b13-0cf0-a65799e26679"],
 " once for every photon in it, so a pattern becomes the rows (or columns) of \
its matrix:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->405213039,ExpressionUUID->"46632a09-cc5f-b0ef-6e10-cc52e5eb0235"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{"ClearAll", "[", "modeList", "]"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"modeList", "[", "occ_List", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"Catenate", " ", "@", " ", 
   RowBox[{"MapIndexed", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"ConstantArray", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"First", "[", "#2", "]"}], ",", " ", "#1"}], "]"}], " ", 
      "&"}], ",", " ", "occ"}], "]"}]}]}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[11]:= ",
 CellID->2118931930,ExpressionUUID->"6ea251ed-865f-e03c-1652-48b733ebb306"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Verify that every probability in the distribution we collected is the \
squared permanent of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["W", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "25a0bfbe-b1d4-2428-535f-36fbc0668f72"],
 ", divided by that normalization:"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1039057480,ExpressionUUID->"04786d0e-016a-d127-2c07-8568b01ca00d"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"Max", "[", 
   RowBox[{"Abs", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Lookup", "[", 
         RowBox[{"probs", ",", " ", 
          RowBox[{"Ket", "[", "#", "]"}], ",", " ", "0"}], "]"}], " ", "-", 
        "\n", "      ", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"Abs", "[", 
           RowBox[{"Permanent", "[", 
            RowBox[{"W", "[", 
             RowBox[{"[", 
              RowBox[{
               RowBox[{"modeList", "[", "#", "]"}], ",", " ", 
               RowBox[{"{", 
                RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "2", ",", " ", "3"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], 
             "]"}], "]"}], "]"}], "^", "2"}], "/", 
         RowBox[{"(", 
          RowBox[{"Times", " ", "@@", " ", 
           RowBox[{"(", 
            RowBox[{"#", "!"}], ")"}]}], ")"}]}]}], ")"}], " ", "&"}], " ", "/@",
      "\n", "    ", 
     RowBox[{"FrobeniusSolve", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
        "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "1", ",", " ", "1"}], 
        "}"}], ",", " ", "3"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], "]"}], " ", "<", " ", 
  RowBox[{"10", "^", 
   RowBox[{"-", "12"}]}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[12]:= ",
 CellID->1411198270,ExpressionUUID->"2d15b43a-cd49-5244-5b2a-2e67e4fc20ae"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "As one can see, the whole distribution is a table of permanents. ",
 Cell[BoxData["QuantumInterferometer"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2d1ff276-b82a-c3ca-5b24-f43039dc9110"],
 " computes it exactly this way, straight from the transfer matrix, without \
ever building and evolving a giant list of every way the photons could be \
arranged. So to imitate the sampler, a classical computer must evaluate a \
permanent for every pattern it wants a probability for. The next question is \
the only one that matters: how expensive is one permanent?"
}], "Text",
 CellID->1703453532,ExpressionUUID->"14fd1512-f616-8834-3c82-8d5f83a0db1e"],

Cell["The Cost: One Permanent Doubles With Every Photon", "Section",
 CellID->1477609488,ExpressionUUID->"66c67103-72a1-9427-0efe-696d0755ff0d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Take the hardest honest case, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "48c794f4-84f6-2bfc-78fc-0dbe2c8379c6"],
 " photons entering ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "58ee7ecb-1370-1005-0896-7795a605636f"],
 " ports of an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "11d04a92-cbef-a8a5-47e0-63dfed9bfa8f"],
 "-mode network, so the matrix behind one amplitude is the full ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], "\[Times]", 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7d9821c9-0e61-bc6d-2ba7-b893ab1506b7"],
 " transfer matrix. The permanent has no shortcut like the one the \
determinant enjoys: the best known exact methods add up a number of terms \
that grows like ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox["2", 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3181a460-bc62-f1ee-67be-3e4a3514c334"],
 ". In other words, every photon added roughly doubles the work for a single \
amplitude."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1030824397,ExpressionUUID->"3937e1d4-04c3-e55a-68cc-f89ead77cfa0"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Define a function that builds a random ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "6b45e796-37a7-eecc-197a-fed04253b416"],
 "-mode network and times the permanent of its transfer matrix, the matrix \
behind the amplitude of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "5e187950-8ca2-2ab2-0638-711a25577898"],
 " photons entering one per port (",
 Cell[BoxData["RepeatedTiming"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "250a2d7c-9b81-05b0-5309-c5261e346f9a"],
 " evaluates the permanent several times and returns a trimmed mean):"
}], "Text",
 CellID->100031176,ExpressionUUID->"15a9104d-5625-6af3-4356-8a1763d038d9"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{"ClearAll", "[", "permanentTime", "]"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"permanentTime", "[", "n_", "]"}], " ", ":=", " ", 
  RowBox[{"Block", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", "w", "}"}], ",", "\n", "   ", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"SeedRandom", "[", "n", "]"}], ";", "\n", "   ", 
     RowBox[{"w", " ", "=", " ", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"QuantumInterferometer", "[", 
        RowBox[{"\"\<Random\>\"", "[", "n", "]"}], "]"}], 
       "[", "\"\<Unitary\>\"", "]"}]}], ";", "\n", "   ", 
     RowBox[{"ClearSystemCache", "[", "]"}], ";", "\n", "   ", 
     RowBox[{"First", " ", "@", " ", 
      RowBox[{"RepeatedTiming", "[", 
       RowBox[{"Permanent", "[", 
        RowBox[{"w", ",", " ", 
         RowBox[{"Method", " ", "->", " ", "\"\<Glynn\>\""}]}], "]"}], 
       "]"}]}]}]}], "]"}]}]}], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[13]:= ",
 CellID->144223354,ExpressionUUID->"58c17d8b-1b7e-6f79-08ff-74d59e0b3243"],

Cell["\<\
Time it for 10 through 20 photons (this cell takes about a minute):\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1443527617,ExpressionUUID->"43821c38-6720-b4f9-71b1-966212da1ec3"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"permanentTimes", " ", "=", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"n", ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"permanentTime", "[", "n", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"n", ",", " ", "10", ",", " ", "20"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], 
  ";"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[14]:= ",
 CellID->311511756,ExpressionUUID->"341c54d9-3079-6bba-5c24-4d83410c3980"],

Cell["Plot the timings on a logarithmic scale:", "Text",
 CellID->1381425643,ExpressionUUID->"1dbf9c2b-d84a-d79b-4b44-1674d9fcbde1"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"ListLogPlot", "[", 
  RowBox[{"permanentTimes", ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Joined", " ", "->", " ", "True"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"PlotMarkers", " ", "->", " ", "Automatic"}], ",", "\n", " ", 
   RowBox[{"Frame", " ", "->", " ", "True"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"GridLines", " ", "->", " ", "Automatic"}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"AspectRatio", " ", "->", " ", 
    RowBox[{"1", "/", "2"}]}], ",", " ", 
   RowBox[{"ImageSize", " ", "->", " ", "Large"}], ",", "\n", " ", 
   RowBox[{"FrameLabel", " ", "->", " ", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"\"\<photons n\>\"", ",", " ", "\"\<seconds\>\""}], "}"}]}], ",",
    "\n", " ", 
   RowBox[{
   "PlotLabel", " ", "->", 
    " ", "\"\<Time to compute one n\[Times]n permanent\>\""}]}], 
  "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[15]:= ",
 CellID->377280352,ExpressionUUID->"0f449897-1a0e-51e7-357b-11d19fbb23cd"],

Cell["\<\
As one can see, the points fall on a straight line, and a straight line on a \
logarithmic scale means the time grows exponentially with the number of \
photons.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1717363484,ExpressionUUID->"6d5a63ab-93b9-14f3-1b7a-7af5264c7ed9"],

Cell["\<\
Compute the average factor by which the time grows with each added photon, as \
the geometric mean over the range:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1781880651,ExpressionUUID->"5077c06e-c394-b43c-000c-da4bf6261f06"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"(", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"permanentTimes", "[", 
     RowBox[{"[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"-", "1"}], ",", " ", "2"}], "]"}], "]"}], "/", 
    RowBox[{"permanentTimes", "[", 
     RowBox[{"[", 
      RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "2"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ")"}], "^", 
  RowBox[{"(", 
   RowBox[{"1", "/", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Length", "[", "permanentTimes", "]"}], " ", "-", " ", "1"}], 
     ")"}]}], ")"}]}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[16]:= ",
 CellID->1536527082,ExpressionUUID->"07359582-e823-44a1-2eca-0cef89d62e8b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The factor sits near two: each added photon roughly doubles the cost of a \
single amplitude, exactly as the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox["2", 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "7c239bd0-3ff5-3b18-25d0-129ab9818662"],
 " growth predicts. At that rate a few dozen photons put one amplitude beyond \
a laptop, and a hundred beyond any computer, even though the physics is \
nothing more than photons sliding between modes."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1621535814,ExpressionUUID->"68c76573-72ce-0c1a-18fd-7d3d077b5760"],

Cell["Fermions Are Easy: The Sign Under Exchange", "Section",
 CellID->1193935837,ExpressionUUID->"6184d2f2-049e-728e-17bb-cbbcad2b40d4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Here is the sharpest way to see that the permanent is the whole difficulty. \
Identical fermions, electrons rather than photons, obey the same linear \
optics, but their histories pick up a minus sign when two are exchanged. That \
single sign turns the permanent into a determinant. And the determinant, \
because its alternating signs allow elimination, is cheap: its cost grows \
like ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], "3"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4fd967ef-5008-0b29-75e7-7ec961bd5673"],
 ", not ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox["2", 
   StyleBox["n", "TI"]]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "19bb1c5e-57ab-6e36-4f58-9618625e351c"],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellID->105329933,ExpressionUUID->"5c8dcc56-7aa9-964a-04b2-e6107f5dfc90"],

Cell["\<\
Time the determinant of a thousand-by-thousand random matrix, the fermionic \
amplitude for a thousand particles:\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1234416869,ExpressionUUID->"30baa1c8-66ad-75e0-6059-389213585fca"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Block", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"a", " ", "=", " ", 
     RowBox[{"RandomComplex", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"-", "1"}], " ", "-", " ", "I"}], ",", " ", 
         RowBox[{"1", " ", "+", " ", "I"}]}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"1000", ",", " ", "1000"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", 
   "\n", " ", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"ClearSystemCache", "[", "]"}], ";", " ", 
    RowBox[{"First", " ", "@", " ", 
     RowBox[{"AbsoluteTiming", "[", 
      RowBox[{"Det", "[", "a", "]"}], "]"}]}]}]}], "]"}]], "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[17]:= ",
 CellID->240380995,ExpressionUUID->"52535901-cb26-8b9c-0261-516aeed0d9e6"],

Cell["\<\
As one can see, the amplitude of a thousand identical fermions costs less \
than the permanent of a couple of dozen photons we timed just above. The \
physics of the two particles is identical up to one sign under exchange, and \
that sign is the entire distance between a computation that finishes before \
you lift your hands from the keyboard and one that outlasts the universe.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->1771870337,ExpressionUUID->"323a4b1c-b138-a791-63da-6359c0b20dfb"],

Cell["Where This Leaves Us", "Section",
 CellID->305160771,ExpressionUUID->"7421cf44-a1ac-4319-1dde-e721d0592e63"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The argument closes on itself: a boson sampler produces detection patterns \
whose probabilities are permanents of its transfer matrix, ",
 Cell[BoxData["QuantumInterferometer"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "511b3347-8d7d-ab60-0738-ab212409f64a"],
 " hands us those probabilities directly, each one is a permanent we verified \
by hand, the cost of a permanent doubles with every photon, and the fermionic \
version with its one extra sign collapses to an easy determinant. Boson \
sampling is hard for exactly one reason, and now you have computed it: the \
permanent has no shortcut, and the identical photons are computing one every \
time they land."
}], "Text",
 CellID->1896945989,ExpressionUUID->"36c19c85-764d-0b01-5efe-15ef03f8566b"],

Cell["\<\
From here the natural next step is to ask what happens when the photons are \
not perfectly identical or when some are lost, since a real experiment is \
never ideal, and whether squeezed light in place of single photons (Gaussian \
boson sampling, where hafnians replace permanents) changes the story. But the \
core is already in your hands, and it is a single sentence: the permanent is \
why it is hard.\
\>", "Text",
 CellID->653417278,ExpressionUUID->"6815cd54-0cd7-196f-182a-e51ea5626bb5"]
},
FrontEndVersion->"15.0 for Mac OS X ARM (64-bit) (July 2, 2026)",
StyleDefinitions->"Default.nb",
ExpressionUUID->"519775d4-d545-25f2-07ad-6c91dcf18fd8"
]
